IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clca-dates`

-- Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), chapter 8A (institutional child abuse), with the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21C it
-- relies on: the ACT's equivalents of the national child abuse reforms (no limitation period; organisations' duty and
-- vicarious liability; proper defendants for unincorporated bodies; setting aside old settlements).
--
-- SEAT: the person who was abused as a child and brings, or wants to bring, a claim for it (the plaintiff; under part
-- 8A.3, the applicant). "Is my claim a child abuse claim?", "Is there a time limit?", "Was the organisation responsible
-- for me?", "On what grounds can I hold it liable?", "Whom do I sue when the body is unincorporated?", "By when must the
-- body tell the court about its trusts?", "How much can I get from the trustee?", "Can my old settlement be set aside?",
-- "Must I pay back what I was paid?". The organisation, the unincorporated body, its trustee and the court get no seat:
-- what they did, and what the court is satisfied of, are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts. No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The one duty with a deadline here that the
-- claimant meets (the body's 28 days to tell the court about its related trusts, s 114E (3)) is a regulative rule run
-- inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: ss 114A, 114AA, 114B, 114BA; 114BB, 114BBA, 114BBB-114BBD, 114BC, 114BE with s 262; 114C (1) as
-- scope, 114D (4), 114E (3)-(5), 114F, 114G (3), (5); 114I-114M; Limitation Act 1985 s 21C. The rest: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), A2002-40, Republication No 77 (R77), effective 16 May 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A2002-40.txt; Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), A1985-66, R28, effective 26 November 2025,
-- deposited at ../../../limitation-act-1985/registers/source-bundle/A1985-66.txt. The Civil Law (Wrongs) Regulation 2003
-- is not deposited: where ss 114BBC, 114BBD (4) (f) and 114BC (2) (b), (3) let it prescribe, nothing prescribed is used.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS. Answers follow the rulings
-- on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): "(the text's no)" and "NOTHING-kind" mark the two kinds of no.

§ `Chapter 8A -- institutional child abuse: the questions of the person abused as a child`

§§ `Part 8A.1 -- Is my claim a child abuse claim, and is there a time limit`

-- s 114A: child abuse claim "means" a claim for personal injury arising from child abuse to which the person was
-- subjected when a child. s 114AA (1): child abuse "means" physical or sexual abuse of a child. (2): physical abuse does
-- not include conduct justified or excused under a territory law (a flat negative); sexual abuse "includes" an offence
-- or misconduct of a sexual nature (an open list: other sexual abuse is NOTHING-kind, fork F1).
DECLARE `What was done to me as a child` IS ONE OF
    `physical abuse`
    `an offence of a sexual nature`
    `misconduct of a sexual nature`
    `other sexual abuse`
    `something that was neither physical nor sexual abuse`

DECLARE `Is it a child abuse claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a child abuse claim (s 114A, s 114AA)`
    `no: physical abuse does not include conduct justified or excused under a law applying in the ACT (the text's no, s 114AA (2))`
    `no: a child abuse claim is a claim for personal injury arising from physical or sexual abuse suffered as a child (the text's no, s 114A, s 114AA (1))`
    `sexual abuse includes offences and misconduct of a sexual nature, and the list is not closed; whether this counts is not said (NOTHING-kind, s 114AA (2))`

@export Is my claim a child abuse claim under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) (ss 114A, 114AA)? It is if you claim for a personal injury arising from physical or sexual abuse you were subjected to as a child. Chapter 8A, the no-time-limit rule and the notice rule for child abuse claims all turn on this.
GIVEN `for personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for a personal injury (including a psychological or psychiatric injury)
      `what was done` IS A `What was done to me as a child` @desc What was done to you: physical abuse; an offence of a sexual nature; misconduct of a sexual nature; other sexual abuse; or something that was neither physical nor sexual abuse
      `justified or excused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For physical abuse: whether the conduct was justified or excused under a law applying in the ACT (otherwise answer no)
      `I was a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a child (under 18) when it was done to you
      `injury arises from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury arises from it
GIVETH A `Is it a child abuse claim`
`s 114A -- is my claim a child abuse claim` `for personal injury` `what was done` `justified or excused` `I was a child` `injury arises from it` MEANS
    IF NOT (`for personal injury` AND `I was a child` AND `injury arises from it`)
    THEN `no: a child abuse claim is a claim for personal injury arising from physical or sexual abuse suffered as a child (the text's no, s 114A, s 114AA (1))`
    ELSE CONSIDER `what was done`
         WHEN `physical abuse` THEN
             IF `justified or excused`
             THEN `no: physical abuse does not include conduct justified or excused under a law applying in the ACT (the text's no, s 114AA (2))`
             ELSE `yes: a child abuse claim (s 114A, s 114AA)`
         WHEN `an offence of a sexual nature` THEN `yes: a child abuse claim (s 114A, s 114AA)`
         WHEN `misconduct of a sexual nature` THEN `yes: a child abuse claim (s 114A, s 114AA)`
         WHEN `other sexual abuse` THEN `sexual abuse includes offences and misconduct of a sexual nature, and the list is not closed; whether this counts is not said (NOTHING-kind, s 114AA (2))`
         WHEN `something that was neither physical nor sexual abuse` THEN `no: a child abuse claim is a claim for personal injury arising from physical or sexual abuse suffered as a child (the text's no, s 114A, s 114AA (1))`

-- Limitation Act 1985 s 21C (1), (2): an action for the death or personal injury of a person, if the cause of action
-- substantially arises from child abuse (as s 114AA (1) defines it) to which the person was subjected as a child,
-- "subjected" including witnessing (s 21C (4)), is maintainable at any time and not subject to any limitation period,
-- whatever the form of action and whenever it accrued. (3): the court's powers, such as a permanent stay where the
-- passage of time makes a fair trial impossible, are kept. Brought in because s 51 note 2 and s 114J (1) (a) rest on it
-- (NOTES.md, departure D1). Outside s 21C the ordinary periods apply: the Limitation Act's own encodings say which.
DECLARE `Is there a time limit on my claim` IS ONE OF
    `no time limit: the action may be brought at any time and no limitation period applies (Limitation Act 1985 s 21C (2)); the court may still stay a proceeding that can no longer be tried fairly (s 21C (3))`
    `s 21C does not apply: the ordinary limitation periods of the Limitation Act 1985 apply (the text's no, s 21C (1))`

@export Is there a time limit on my claim for injury from abuse I suffered or witnessed as a child (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21C, read with Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 s 51 note 2)? No limitation period applies to a claim for death or personal injury that substantially arises from physical or sexual abuse the person was subjected to (or witnessed) as a child, whenever it happened.
GIVEN `for death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for your personal injury (or for the death of the person abused)
      `what was done` IS A `What was done to me as a child` @desc What was done (to you, or in your sight)
      `justified or excused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For physical abuse: whether the conduct was justified or excused under a law applying in the ACT (otherwise answer no)
      `subjected or witnessed as a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were subjected to it, or witnessed it, when you were a child (under 18)
      `substantially arises from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your cause of action substantially arises from it
GIVETH A `Is there a time limit on my claim`
`Limitation Act s 21C -- is there a time limit on my claim` `for death or personal injury` `what was done` `justified or excused` `subjected or witnessed as a child` `substantially arises from it` MEANS
    IF `for death or personal injury` AND `subjected or witnessed as a child` AND `substantially arises from it` AND `child abuse as s 114AA defines it`
    THEN `no time limit: the action may be brought at any time and no limitation period applies (Limitation Act 1985 s 21C (2)); the court may still stay a proceeding that can no longer be tried fairly (s 21C (3))`
    ELSE `s 21C does not apply: the ordinary limitation periods of the Limitation Act 1985 apply (the text's no, s 21C (1))`
    WHERE
        -- "other sexual abuse" is NOTHING-kind under s 114AA; for s 21C it is read as outside (fork F1).
        `child abuse as s 114AA defines it` MEANS
            CONSIDER `what was done`
            WHEN `physical abuse` THEN NOT `justified or excused`
            WHEN `an offence of a sexual nature` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `misconduct of a sexual nature` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Part 8A.1A -- The organisation's liability`

-- s 114BB: organisation "includes" an administrative unit, a territory authority, an unincorporated body (an open
-- list: other organisations are asked as a fact). s 114BBA (1): an organisation is responsible for a child "if" it, or
-- any part of it, exercises care, supervision or authority over the child; (2) if it delegates that, in whole or in
-- part, to another organisation, each is responsible.
DECLARE `Who it is` IS ONE OF
    `an ACT administrative unit (a government directorate)`
    `a territory authority`
    `an unincorporated body (such as a club, association or church body that is not incorporated)`
    `another organisation (such as a company, incorporated association, school or religious body)`
    `an individual, not an organisation`

DECLARE `Is it responsible for me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it exercised care, supervision or authority over you (s 114BBA (1))`
    `yes: it delegated care, supervision or authority over you to another organisation, so each is responsible (s 114BBA (2))`
    `no: it neither exercised nor delegated care, supervision or authority over you (the text's no, s 114BBA)`
    `part 8A.1A speaks of organisations; it does not say whether an individual is one (NOTHING-kind, s 114BB)`

@export Was the organisation responsible for me when I was a child (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114BB, 114BBA)? It was if it, or any part of it, exercised care, supervision or authority over you, or handed that over (wholly or partly) to another organisation. The duty to prevent child abuse and vicarious liability both start here.
GIVEN `who it is` IS A `Who it is` @desc What the body is
      `exercised care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it, or any part of it, exercised care, supervision or authority over you
      `delegated care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it delegated (handed over), wholly or partly, the exercise of care, supervision or authority over you to another organisation
GIVETH A `Is it responsible for me`
`s 114BBA -- is the organisation responsible for me` `who it is` `exercised care` `delegated care` MEANS
    IF `who it is` EQUALS `an individual, not an organisation`
    THEN `part 8A.1A speaks of organisations; it does not say whether an individual is one (NOTHING-kind, s 114BB)`
    ELSE IF `exercised care`
         THEN `yes: it exercised care, supervision or authority over you (s 114BBA (1))`
         ELSE IF `delegated care`
              THEN `yes: it delegated care, supervision or authority over you to another organisation, so each is responsible (s 114BBA (2))`
              ELSE `no: it neither exercised nor delegated care, supervision or authority over you (the text's no, s 114BBA)`

-- s 114BBC (1): an individual associated with an organisation "includes" (a) an office holder, officer, owner,
-- employee, agent, volunteer or contractor; (b) for a religious organisation, a religious leader or other member of its
-- personnel, ordained or not; (c) an individual prescribed by regulation (none deposited). (3): an individual associated
-- with an organisation to which it delegated responsibility for the child is taken to be associated with it too. An
-- open list: anyone else is NOTHING-kind. s 114BBC (2) (a regulation may say who is not associated): none deposited.
DECLARE `Is the abuser associated with it` IS ONE OF
    `yes: an office holder, officer, owner, employee, agent, volunteer or contractor of the organisation (s 114BBC (1) (a))`
    `yes: a religious leader or other member of the personnel of a religious organisation (s 114BBC (1) (b))`
    `yes: associated with the organisation it delegated responsibility for you to (s 114BBC (3))`
    `s 114BBC lists who is associated without closing the list; it does not say whether this person is (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Was the person who abused me associated with the organisation (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114BBC)? This includes its office holders, officers, owners, employees, agents, volunteers and contractors, a religious organisation's leaders and other personnel, and people associated with an organisation it handed responsibility for you to.
GIVEN `listed role` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was an office holder, officer, owner, employee, agent, volunteer or contractor of the organisation
      `religious organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a religious organisation
      `religious personnel` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was a religious leader (such as a priest or minister) or other member of its personnel, ordained or not
      `associated with the delegate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation delegated responsibility for you to another organisation, and the abuser was associated with that other organisation
GIVETH A `Is the abuser associated with it`
`s 114BBC -- is the abuser associated with the organisation` `listed role` `religious organisation` `religious personnel` `associated with the delegate` MEANS
    IF `listed role`
    THEN `yes: an office holder, officer, owner, employee, agent, volunteer or contractor of the organisation (s 114BBC (1) (a))`
    ELSE IF `religious organisation` AND `religious personnel`
         THEN `yes: a religious leader or other member of the personnel of a religious organisation (s 114BBC (1) (b))`
         ELSE IF `associated with the delegate`
              THEN `yes: associated with the organisation it delegated responsibility for you to (s 114BBC (3))`
              ELSE `s 114BBC lists who is associated without closing the list; it does not say whether this person is (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 114BC (1): employee "includes" an individual akin to an employee; (2) akin "if" (a) the individual carries out
-- activities that are part of the organisation's ordinary activities and for its benefit, or (b) a prescribed
-- circumstance applies (none deposited). Outside both: NOTHING-kind (the common law meaning of employee is borrowed and
-- asked as a fact).
DECLARE `Is the abuser its employee` IS ONE OF
    `yes: an employee of the organisation (s 114BC (1))`
    `yes: akin to an employee, carrying out activities that are part of its ordinary activities and for its benefit (s 114BC (2) (a))`
    `s 114BC does not say whether this person counts as an employee (NOTHING-kind)`

@export For vicarious liability, was the person who abused me an employee of the organisation, or akin to one (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114BC)? Someone who is not employed counts if they carried out activities that are part of the organisation's ordinary activities and for its benefit.
GIVEN `employed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was employed by the organisation
      `ordinary activities` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser carried out activities that are part of the ordinary activities carried out by the organisation
      `for its benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those activities were for the benefit of the organisation
GIVETH A `Is the abuser its employee`
`s 114BC -- is the abuser an employee of the organisation` `employed` `ordinary activities` `for its benefit` MEANS
    IF `employed`
    THEN `yes: an employee of the organisation (s 114BC (1))`
    ELSE IF `ordinary activities` AND `for its benefit`
         THEN `yes: akin to an employee, carrying out activities that are part of its ordinary activities and for its benefit (s 114BC (2) (a))`
         ELSE `s 114BC does not say whether this person counts as an employee (NOTHING-kind)`

-- The two statutory routes against an organisation, side by side.
-- s 114BBD (2), (3): an organisation responsible for a child must take reasonable precautions to prevent an individual
-- associated with it from perpetrating child abuse against the child in connection with its responsibility; if such an
-- individual does, it is taken to have breached its duty of care unless it establishes that it took reasonable
-- precautions. s 114BBB: only for abuse on or after the day division 8A.1A.2 commenced, 16 May 2026 (finding 1).
-- s 114BE (1): an organisation responsible for a child is vicariously liable for child abuse perpetrated against the
-- child by its employee if the apparent performance of a role in which it placed the employee supplied the occasion and
-- the employee took advantage of it; s 114BA: whenever the abuse happened; s 262: also in proceedings pending on
-- 13 November 2025 (fork F4). (3): the common law of vicarious liability is untouched, so where neither route holds the
-- answer is NOTHING-kind: the common law may still give a claim.
DECLARE `On what grounds can I hold it liable` IS ONE OF
    `both: it is taken to have breached its duty to prevent the abuse (s 114BBD (3)), and it is vicariously liable for its employee's abuse (s 114BE (1))`
    `the duty route: it is taken to have breached its duty to prevent the abuse unless it proves it took reasonable precautions, and here it has not (s 114BBD (3))`
    `the vicarious route: it is vicariously liable for its employee's abuse, whenever it happened (s 114BE (1), s 114BA)`
    `neither statutory route holds on these facts; the common law of negligence and vicarious liability still applies (NOTHING-kind, s 114BE (3))`

@export On what grounds can I hold the organisation liable for the abuse I suffered as a child (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114BBB, 114BBD, 114BE)? For abuse on or after 16 May 2026, an organisation responsible for you is taken to have breached its duty if someone associated with it abused you in connection with that responsibility, unless it proves reasonable precautions. Whenever the abuse happened, it is vicariously liable for an employee (or someone akin) whose role supplied the occasion.
GIVEN `abuse happened on` IS A DATE @desc The day the abuse happened (or is alleged to have happened); if it went on over a period, its first day
      `organisation responsible for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation was responsible for you (it, or part of it, exercised or delegated care, supervision or authority over you)
      `abuser associated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was associated with the organisation (office holder, officer, owner, employee, agent, volunteer, contractor, religious personnel, and the like)
      `in connection with its responsibility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuse was in connection with the organisation's responsibility for you
      `it proves reasonable precautions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation establishes (proves to the court) that it took reasonable precautions to prevent the abuse
      `abuser employee or akin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was its employee, or akin to one
      `role supplied the occasion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the apparent performance of a role the organisation placed the abuser in supplied the occasion for the abuse (the court looks at whether it gave them authority, power or control over you, your trust, or the ability to achieve intimacy with you)
      `took advantage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser took advantage of that occasion to abuse you
GIVETH A `On what grounds can I hold it liable`
`ch 8A -- on what grounds can I hold the organisation liable` `abuse happened on` `organisation responsible for me` `abuser associated` `in connection with its responsibility` `it proves reasonable precautions` `abuser employee or akin` `role supplied the occasion` `took advantage` MEANS
    IF `the duty route holds` AND `the vicarious route holds`
    THEN `both: it is taken to have breached its duty to prevent the abuse (s 114BBD (3)), and it is vicariously liable for its employee's abuse (s 114BE (1))`
    ELSE IF `the duty route holds`
         THEN `the duty route: it is taken to have breached its duty to prevent the abuse unless it proves it took reasonable precautions, and here it has not (s 114BBD (3))`
         ELSE IF `the vicarious route holds`
              THEN `the vicarious route: it is vicariously liable for its employee's abuse, whenever it happened (s 114BE (1), s 114BA)`
              ELSE `neither statutory route holds on these facts; the common law of negligence and vicarious liability still applies (NOTHING-kind, s 114BE (3))`
    WHERE
        `the duty route holds` MEANS
                (NOT (`abuse happened on` `falls before` `the day division 8A.1A.2 commenced`))
            AND `organisation responsible for me`
            AND `abuser associated`
            AND `in connection with its responsibility`
            AND (NOT `it proves reasonable precautions`)
        `the vicarious route holds` MEANS
                `organisation responsible for me`
            AND `abuser employee or akin`
            AND `role supplied the occasion`
            AND `took advantage`

§§ `Part 8A.2 -- Suing an unincorporated body (arises only if the body that was responsible for you is not incorporated)`

-- s 114B: related trust "means" a trust controlled by the unincorporated body which the body uses to conduct its
-- activities; (2) controlled "if" any of (a)-(g).
DECLARE `Is it a related trust` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a related trust of the body: it controls the trust and uses it to conduct its activities (s 114B)`
    `no: the body does not use the trust to conduct its activities (the text's no, s 114B (1))`
    `no: the body does not control the trust in any of the ways s 114B (2) lists (the text's no)`

@export Is this trust a related trust of the unincorporated body I am suing for child abuse (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114B)? It is if the body uses the trust to conduct its activities and controls it in one of the ways the Act lists. A related trust can be made the defendant (s 114E).
GIVEN `uses the trust for its activities` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body uses the trust to conduct its activities
      `controls income or property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has direct or indirect power to control how the trust's income is applied or its property distributed
      `can enjoy property or income` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has direct or indirect power to obtain the beneficial enjoyment of the trust's property or income, with or without anyone's consent
      `appoints or removes trustees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has direct or indirect power to appoint or remove a trustee
      `appoints or removes beneficiaries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has direct or indirect power to appoint or remove beneficiaries
      `trustee follows its wishes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trustee is accustomed, or obliged (formally or informally), to act on the body's directions, instructions or wishes
      `decides other trust decisions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has direct or indirect power to decide the outcome of any other decision about the trust's operations
      `a member has such a power under the deed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a member of the body has any of those powers under the trust deed
GIVETH A `Is it a related trust`
`s 114B -- is the trust a related trust of the body` `uses the trust for its activities` `controls income or property` `can enjoy property or income` `appoints or removes trustees` `appoints or removes beneficiaries` `trustee follows its wishes` `decides other trust decisions` `a member has such a power under the deed` MEANS
    IF NOT `uses the trust for its activities`
    THEN `no: the body does not use the trust to conduct its activities (the text's no, s 114B (1))`
    ELSE IF `controls income or property` OR `can enjoy property or income` OR `appoints or removes trustees` OR `appoints or removes beneficiaries` OR `trustee follows its wishes` OR `decides other trust decisions` OR `a member has such a power under the deed`
         THEN `yes: a related trust of the body: it controls the trust and uses it to conduct its activities (s 114B)`
         ELSE `no: the body does not control the trust in any of the ways s 114B (2) lists (the text's no)`

-- s 114F (1): a proceeding for a child abuse claim may be commenced or continued against an unincorporated body in its
-- own name before a nomination (s 114D (2)) or an appointment (s 114E (4)); (2) the court may make interlocutory orders
-- against it as if it were incorporated; (3) a later nomination or appointment does not relieve the body or its members
-- of those orders. s 114D (4): once the body nominates, the claim may be brought against the nominee, who is the
-- defendant for all purposes and may be found liable for what the body would owe if incorporated. s 114E (5): the same
-- for a related trust the court appoints. s 114C (1): part 8A.2 applies whatever the body's constitution or membership.
-- Part 8A.2 is limited to child abuse claims against unincorporated bodies, with no "only": outside it, NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Has a defendant been named for the body` IS ONE OF
    `not yet`
    `the body nominated a defendant`
    `the court appointed a related trust as defendant`

DECLARE `Whom do I sue` IS ONE OF
    `the body itself, in its own name: you may start or continue the proceeding before a defendant is nominated or appointed, and the court may make interlocutory orders against the body as if it were incorporated (s 114F (1), (2))`
    `the nominee: your claim may be brought against the entity the body nominated, which is the defendant for the body for all purposes and can be found liable for what the body would owe if incorporated (s 114D (4)); orders already made against the body still bind it (s 114F (3))`
    `the appointed trust: your claim may be brought or continued against the related trust the court appointed, which is the defendant for the body for all purposes and can be found liable for what the body would owe if incorporated (s 114E (5)); orders already made against the body still bind it (s 114F (3))`
    `part 8A.2 deals only with child abuse claims against unincorporated bodies; it says nothing here (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Whom do I sue for my child abuse claim when the body responsible is not incorporated, such as a church, club or association (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114C, 114D, 114E, 114F)? You may sue the body in its own name until it nominates a defendant or the court appoints a related trust; after that, the nominee or the trust.
GIVEN `child abuse claim against an unincorporated body` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a child abuse claim and the body you are suing is unincorporated (whether or not it has a written constitution or fixed membership)
      `named so far` IS A `Has a defendant been named for the body` @desc Whether a defendant has been named for the body: not yet; the body nominated one; or the court appointed a related trust
GIVETH A `Whom do I sue`
`s 114F -- whom do I sue for the unincorporated body` `child abuse claim against an unincorporated body` `named so far` MEANS
    IF NOT `child abuse claim against an unincorporated body`
    THEN `part 8A.2 deals only with child abuse claims against unincorporated bodies; it says nothing here (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `named so far`
         WHEN `not yet` THEN `the body itself, in its own name: you may start or continue the proceeding before a defendant is nominated or appointed, and the court may make interlocutory orders against the body as if it were incorporated (s 114F (1), (2))`
         WHEN `the body nominated a defendant` THEN `the nominee: your claim may be brought against the entity the body nominated, which is the defendant for the body for all purposes and can be found liable for what the body would owe if incorporated (s 114D (4)); orders already made against the body still bind it (s 114F (3))`
         WHEN `the court appointed a related trust as defendant` THEN `the appointed trust: your claim may be brought or continued against the related trust the court appointed, which is the defendant for the body for all purposes and can be found liable for what the body would owe if incorporated (s 114E (5)); orders already made against the body still bind it (s 114F (3))`

-- s 114E (3): if the plaintiff applies for a related trust to be appointed defendant, the unincorporated body must,
-- within 28 days of the application being made, notify the court about any related trusts and the financial capacity of
-- each. A duty with a deadline: a regulative rule (MUST ... WITHIN ... LEST BREACH). The Act states no consequence of a
-- breach; the court may in any case appoint a related trust it was not told about (s 114E (4)). Time is counted in day
-- serials: the rule's deadline is the last day's serial and events sit at their days' serials.
DECLARE `Part 8A.2 party` IS ONE OF `the unincorporated body`
DECLARE `Part 8A.2 step` IS ONE OF `notify the court of its related trusts and their financial capacity`

GIVEN `the last day serial` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Part 8A.2 party` `Part 8A.2 step`
`the body must notify the court of its related trusts` `the last day serial` MEANS
    PARTY `the unincorporated body`
    MUST `notify the court of its related trusts and their financial capacity`
    WITHIN `the last day serial`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the unincorporated body` BECAUSE "no notice to the court of the body's related trusts and their financial capacity within 28 days of the plaintiff's application (s 114E (3))"

-- The last day: the 28th day after the application was made (fork F3).
@export By when must the unincorporated body tell the court about its related trusts after I apply to have one appointed defendant (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114E (3))? Within 28 days of your application: the answer is the last day.
GIVEN `application made on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied to the court for a related trust to be appointed defendant for the body
GIVETH A DATE
`s 114E (3) -- the last day for the body to tell the court about its related trusts` `application made on` MEANS
    `the day that is` 28 `days after` `application made on`

DECLARE `Has the body told the court in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the body told the court about its related trusts within 28 days of your application (s 114E (3))`
    `not yet due: the body has not told the court yet, and its 28 days have not ended (s 114E (3))`
    `no: the body did not tell the court about its related trusts within 28 days of your application, in breach of s 114E (3); the court may still appoint a related trust it was not told about (s 114E (4))`

@export Has the unincorporated body told the court about its related trusts in time (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114E (3))? It must notify the court of any related trusts and the financial capacity of each within 28 days after you apply for a related trust to be appointed defendant.
GIVEN `application made on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied to the court for a related trust to be appointed defendant for the body
      `body notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has notified the court about its related trusts and their financial capacity
      `notified on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Has the body told the court in time`
`s 114E (3) -- has the body told the court in time` `application made on` `body notified` `notified on` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the body must notify the court of its related trusts` (DATE_SERIAL `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `yes: the body told the court about its related trusts within 28 days of your application (s 114E (3))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the day looked at` `falls after` `the last day`
        THEN `no: the body did not tell the court about its related trusts within 28 days of your application, in breach of s 114E (3); the court may still appoint a related trust it was not told about (s 114E (4))`
        ELSE `not yet due: the body has not told the court yet, and its 28 days have not ended (s 114E (3))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `s 114E (3) -- the last day for the body to tell the court about its related trusts` `application made on`
        `the day looked at` MEANS
            IF `body notified`
            THEN `notified on`
            ELSE `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `body notified`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `notified on`)), (EVENT `the unincorporated body` `notify the court of its related trusts and their financial capacity` (DATE_SERIAL `notified on`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `today`))

-- s 114G (3) (a): the trustee of a related trust nominated or appointed as defendant is not liable under part 8A.2 for
-- more than the total value of the trust property. (5): liability includes an unpaid judgment debt, an amount paid in
-- settlement, and costs of the proceeding (an open list: the three are added, anything else is outside this answer).
-- (2) (d): the trustee may apply trust property to pay, and be indemnified from it.
@export What is the most I can recover from the trustee of a related trust that is defendant for the unincorporated body (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114G)? The trustee's liability (judgment debt, settlement amount and costs) is capped at the total value of the trust property. The answer is dollars.
GIVEN `unpaid judgment debt` IS A NUMBER @desc The unpaid judgment debt from your proceeding, in dollars (0 if none)
      `settlement amount` IS A NUMBER @desc Any amount to be paid in settlement of the proceeding, in dollars (0 if none)
      `costs` IS A NUMBER @desc The costs associated with the proceeding that the trustee is liable for, in dollars (0 if none)
      `trust property value` IS A NUMBER @desc The total value of the trust's property, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 114G -- the most I can recover from the trustee` `unpaid judgment debt` `settlement amount` `costs` `trust property value` MEANS
    IF `the liability` GREATER THAN `trust property value`
    THEN `trust property value`
    ELSE `the liability`
    WHERE
        `the liability` MEANS `unpaid judgment debt` PLUS `settlement amount` PLUS `costs`

§§ `Part 8A.3 -- Setting aside an old settlement (arises only if you settled a child abuse claim in the past)`

-- s 114J (1): abuse settlement agreement "means" an agreement (a) that settles a child abuse claim and prevents the
-- exercise of an action on a cause of action to which Limitation Act s 21C applies, and (b) that (i) happened before
-- s 21C commenced (26 August 2016) when a limitation period applying to the cause of action had ended; or (ii) happened
-- before part 8A.3 commenced (10 December 2022) and is not just and reasonable in the circumstances; or (iii) happened
-- before part 8A.1A commenced (13 November 2025) and the abuse was by a person who would have been an employee of an
-- organisation had that part been in force. (2): a period ended even if leave to extend it could have been sought.
-- Whether the agreement is not just and reasonable is the court's judgment, asked as a fact. Where more than one limb
-- holds, the first in the text's order is named (fork F5).
DECLARE `Is it an abuse settlement agreement` IS ONE OF
    `yes: made before 26 August 2016, when the limitation period for your claim had already ended (s 114J (1) (b) (i))`
    `yes: made before 10 December 2022, and not just and reasonable in the circumstances (s 114J (1) (b) (ii))`
    `yes: made before 13 November 2025, and the abuser would have been an employee of an organisation under part 8A.1A (s 114J (1) (b) (iii))`
    `no: it does not settle a child abuse claim and bar a claim to which Limitation Act s 21C applies (the text's no, s 114J (1) (a))`
    `no: none of the three kinds of past settlement in s 114J (1) (b) (the text's no)`

@export Is my past settlement of a child abuse claim an abuse settlement agreement that the court can be asked to set aside (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114J)? It is if it settled your claim and stops you suing, and it was made before 26 August 2016 when your time to sue had run out, or before 10 December 2022 and is not just and reasonable, or before 13 November 2025 where the abuser would now count as the organisation's employee.
GIVEN `settled a child abuse claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement settled a claim for personal injury arising from physical or sexual abuse you were subjected to as a child
      `it stops me suing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement prevents you from bringing an action on your cause of action (for example by a release)
      `cause substantially arises from abuse as a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that cause of action substantially arises from physical or sexual abuse you were subjected to (or witnessed) as a child
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the agreement was made
      `limitation period had ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the agreement was made, a limitation period for your claim had ended (count it as ended even if you could have asked the court for leave to extend it)
      `not just and reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the agreement is not just and reasonable in the circumstances
      `abuser would have been an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser would have been an employee of an organisation (including someone carrying out its ordinary activities for its benefit) under part 8A.1A, had it been in force when the agreement was made
GIVETH A `Is it an abuse settlement agreement`
`s 114J -- is my settlement an abuse settlement agreement` `settled a child abuse claim` `it stops me suing` `cause substantially arises from abuse as a child` `made on` `limitation period had ended` `not just and reasonable` `abuser would have been an employee` MEANS
    IF NOT (`settled a child abuse claim` AND `it stops me suing` AND `cause substantially arises from abuse as a child`)
    THEN `no: it does not settle a child abuse claim and bar a claim to which Limitation Act s 21C applies (the text's no, s 114J (1) (a))`
    ELSE IF (`made on` `falls before` `the day Limitation Act s 21C commenced`) AND `limitation period had ended`
         THEN `yes: made before 26 August 2016, when the limitation period for your claim had already ended (s 114J (1) (b) (i))`
         ELSE IF (`made on` `falls before` `the day part 8A.3 commenced`) AND `not just and reasonable`
              THEN `yes: made before 10 December 2022, and not just and reasonable in the circumstances (s 114J (1) (b) (ii))`
              ELSE IF (`made on` `falls before` `the day part 8A.1A commenced`) AND `abuser would have been an employee`
                   THEN `yes: made before 13 November 2025, and the abuser would have been an employee of an organisation under part 8A.1A (s 114J (1) (b) (iii))`
                   ELSE `no: none of the three kinds of past settlement in s 114J (1) (b) (the text's no)`

-- s 114K (1), (2): a person prevented from suing because of an abuse settlement agreement may begin a proceeding on the
-- cause of action and apply to the court to set the agreement aside. (3): the court may set it aside "if" satisfied that
-- (a) when it was made there were legal barriers to the person being fully compensated, or (b) when the application is
-- made it is, in all the circumstances, not just and reasonable. (4): the court may weigh the amount paid, bargaining
-- positions, and the conduct of the other parties and their lawyers. (5): evidence of settlement negotiations is not
-- excluded by Evidence Act 2011 s 131 (1). The court's satisfaction is asked as facts; "may" stays a discretion.
-- The one "not just and reasonable" fact serves both s 114J (1) (b) (ii) and s 114K (3) (b) (fork F6).
DECLARE `Can my settlement be set aside` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it can be: you may sue and apply to set it aside (s 114K (2)), and the court may set it aside because there were legal barriers to full compensation when it was made (s 114K (3) (a))`
    `yes, it can be: you may sue and apply to set it aside (s 114K (2)), and the court may set it aside because it is not just and reasonable now (s 114K (3) (b))`
    `you may sue and apply (s 114K (2)), but the court may set it aside only if satisfied of legal barriers then or that it is not just and reasonable now, and on these facts it is not (the text's no, s 114K (3))`
    `no: part 8A.3 applies only to an abuse settlement agreement, and this is not one (the text's no, s 114K (1), s 114J)`

@export Can I go to court and have my old settlement of a child abuse claim set aside (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114J, 114K)? If it is an abuse settlement agreement, you may sue and apply to set it aside, and the court may do so if there were legal barriers to full compensation when it was made, or it is not just and reasonable now. Settlement negotiations can be put in evidence (s 114K (5)).
GIVEN `settled a child abuse claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement settled a claim for personal injury arising from physical or sexual abuse you were subjected to as a child
      `it stops me suing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement prevents you from bringing an action on your cause of action
      `cause substantially arises from abuse as a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that cause of action substantially arises from physical or sexual abuse you were subjected to (or witnessed) as a child
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the agreement was made
      `limitation period had ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the agreement was made, a limitation period for your claim had ended (count it as ended even if you could have asked for leave to extend it)
      `not just and reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the agreement is, in all the circumstances, not just and reasonable
      `abuser would have been an employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser would have been an employee of an organisation under part 8A.1A, had it been in force when the agreement was made
      `legal barriers then` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that, when the agreement was made, there were legal barriers to your being fully compensated through a legal cause of action (for example, the time limit had run out, or no organisation could be held liable)
GIVETH A `Can my settlement be set aside`
`s 114K -- can my settlement be set aside` `settled a child abuse claim` `it stops me suing` `cause substantially arises from abuse as a child` `made on` `limitation period had ended` `not just and reasonable` `abuser would have been an employee` `legal barriers then` MEANS
    IF (`is one` EQUALS `no: it does not settle a child abuse claim and bar a claim to which Limitation Act s 21C applies (the text's no, s 114J (1) (a))`) OR (`is one` EQUALS `no: none of the three kinds of past settlement in s 114J (1) (b) (the text's no)`)
    THEN `no: part 8A.3 applies only to an abuse settlement agreement, and this is not one (the text's no, s 114K (1), s 114J)`
    ELSE IF `legal barriers then`
         THEN `yes, it can be: you may sue and apply to set it aside (s 114K (2)), and the court may set it aside because there were legal barriers to full compensation when it was made (s 114K (3) (a))`
         ELSE IF `not just and reasonable`
              THEN `yes, it can be: you may sue and apply to set it aside (s 114K (2)), and the court may set it aside because it is not just and reasonable now (s 114K (3) (b))`
              ELSE `you may sue and apply (s 114K (2)), but the court may set it aside only if satisfied of legal barriers then or that it is not just and reasonable now, and on these facts it is not (the text's no, s 114K (3))`
    WHERE
        `is one` MEANS `s 114J -- is my settlement an abuse settlement agreement` `settled a child abuse claim` `it stops me suing` `cause substantially arises from abuse as a child` `made on` `limitation period had ended` `not just and reasonable` `abuser would have been an employee`

-- s 114L (1): if the court decides to set aside an abuse settlement agreement, it may also set aside a contract, deed or
-- other agreement, or an order or judgment of a court, that gives effect to it. (2): it must not set aside (a) a deed of
-- release accepting a National Redress Scheme offer, or an agreement about a prior payment taken into account in the
-- offer; (b) an agreement so far as it settled a cross-claim between defendants or one defendant indemnified another;
-- (c) a contract of insurance. (2) is a flat negative; (1) is an "if".
DECLARE `The other thing` IS ONE OF
    `a contract, deed or other agreement that gives effect to the settlement`
    `an order or judgment of a court that gives effect to the settlement`
    `a deed of release I signed accepting a National Redress Scheme offer, or an agreement about a prior payment taken into account in that offer`
    `an agreement, so far as it settled a cross-claim between defendants or one defendant indemnified another`
    `a contract of insurance`
    `something that does not give effect to the settlement`

DECLARE `Can it be set aside too` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it may be: the court may also set aside a contract, deed or other agreement that gives effect to the settlement (s 114L (1) (a))`
    `yes, it may be: the court may also set aside an order or judgment of a court that gives effect to the settlement (s 114L (1) (b))`
    `no: the court must not set aside a National Redress Scheme deed of release or an agreement about a prior payment taken into account in the offer (the text's no, s 114L (2) (a))`
    `no: the court must not set aside an agreement so far as it settled a cross-claim between defendants or one defendant indemnified another (the text's no, s 114L (2) (b))`
    `no: the court must not set aside a contract of insurance (the text's no, s 114L (2) (c))`
    `no: the court may set aside other things only if it decides to set aside the settlement itself (the text's no, s 114L (1))`
    `s 114L reaches only things that give effect to the settlement; it says nothing about this (NOTHING-kind)`

@export If the court sets aside my settlement, can it also set aside this other document, order or agreement (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114L)? It may set aside contracts, deeds, agreements, orders and judgments that give effect to the settlement, but never a National Redress Scheme deed of release, a cross-claim or indemnity between defendants, or a contract of insurance.
GIVEN `settlement set aside` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has decided to set aside your abuse settlement agreement
      `the other thing` IS A `The other thing` @desc What the other document, order or agreement is
GIVETH A `Can it be set aside too`
`s 114L -- can the court also set aside this` `settlement set aside` `the other thing` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the other thing`
    WHEN `a deed of release I signed accepting a National Redress Scheme offer, or an agreement about a prior payment taken into account in that offer` THEN `no: the court must not set aside a National Redress Scheme deed of release or an agreement about a prior payment taken into account in the offer (the text's no, s 114L (2) (a))`
    WHEN `an agreement, so far as it settled a cross-claim between defendants or one defendant indemnified another` THEN `no: the court must not set aside an agreement so far as it settled a cross-claim between defendants or one defendant indemnified another (the text's no, s 114L (2) (b))`
    WHEN `a contract of insurance` THEN `no: the court must not set aside a contract of insurance (the text's no, s 114L (2) (c))`
    WHEN `something that does not give effect to the settlement` THEN `s 114L reaches only things that give effect to the settlement; it says nothing about this (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHEN `a contract, deed or other agreement that gives effect to the settlement` THEN
        IF `settlement set aside`
        THEN `yes, it may be: the court may also set aside a contract, deed or other agreement that gives effect to the settlement (s 114L (1) (a))`
        ELSE `no: the court may set aside other things only if it decides to set aside the settlement itself (the text's no, s 114L (1))`
    WHEN `an order or judgment of a court that gives effect to the settlement` THEN
        IF `settlement set aside`
        THEN `yes, it may be: the court may also set aside an order or judgment of a court that gives effect to the settlement (s 114L (1) (b))`
        ELSE `no: the court may set aside other things only if it decides to set aside the settlement itself (the text's no, s 114L (1))`

-- s 114M (1), (2): a settlement set aside is void, but only so far as it relates to the applicant. (3): an amount paid
-- (legal costs and disbursements included) or other consideration given under it is not recoverable despite the
-- agreement being void, and may be taken into account by a court in deciding damages. Before it is set aside, s 114M
-- says nothing (NOTHING-kind): the agreement stands.
DECLARE `Must I pay it back` IS ONE OF
    `no: what you were paid under the settlement (including legal costs or disbursements) is not recoverable from you, though the court may take it into account in deciding your damages; the settlement is void only so far as it relates to you (s 114M)`
    `s 114M speaks only of a settlement the court has set aside; until then the settlement stands (NOTHING-kind)`

@export If my settlement is set aside, must I pay back what I received under it (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 114M)? No: an amount paid or other consideration given under it is not recoverable, though the court may take it into account in deciding your damages.
GIVEN `settlement set aside` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has set aside your abuse settlement agreement
GIVETH A `Must I pay it back`
`s 114M -- must I pay back what I received` `settlement set aside` MEANS
    IF `settlement set aside`
    THEN `no: what you were paid under the settlement (including legal costs or disbursements) is not recoverable from you, though the court may take it into account in deciding your damages; the settlement is void only so far as it relates to you (s 114M)`
    ELSE `s 114M speaks only of a settlement the court has set aside; until then the settlement stands (NOTHING-kind)`
